PATCH: InputManager — add per-button getHeldTime(buttonIndex)

Prepared in the crosspoint-reader firmware tree (tools/patches/README.md explains
why it cannot live there). Not an mbox: apply with `git apply`, not `git am`.

Target repo: https://github.com/Free-Ink/freeink-sdk.git (branch main)
Files:       libs/hardware/InputManager/include/InputManager.h
             libs/hardware/InputManager/src/InputManager.cpp
Base:        e7d3361 ("Fix grayscale rendering and add OEM LUT reference")
             — the commit crosspoint-reader currently pins the submodule to
Apply with:  git apply -p1 <this file>   (from the freeink-sdk repo root)

THE DEFECT
----------
getHeldTime() is a single timer for the whole chord:

    libs/hardware/InputManager/src/InputManager.cpp:246-248
      if (pressedEvents > 0 && currentState == 0) buttonPressStart = currentTime;
    libs/hardware/InputManager/src/InputManager.cpp:405-412
      if (currentState > 0) return millis() - buttonPressStart;

buttonPressStart is stamped only when a button goes down while NOTHING was down,
and a button that joins a chord already in progress does not restart it. Nothing
in a consumer's activity/screen switch resets it either.

On X3/X4 that is reachable, because the two button groups sit on separate ADC
pins and are sampled independently (InputManager.cpp:133-145): BUTTON_ADC_PIN_1
carries BACK/CONFIRM/LEFT/RIGHT, BUTTON_ADC_PIN_2 carries UP/DOWN, and BTN_POWER
is a plain digital pin (InputManager.cpp:147-151). Within one group a second
press is ambiguous on the resistor ladder, but across groups both bits are set.

So: hold a side page-turn button for more than the consumer's long-press
threshold, then press a front button, and the front button's long-press fires
instantly. In crosspoint-reader that turns a normal Back tap into
"go to the file browser" (src/activities/reader/EpubReaderActivity.cpp:476,
threshold 1000 ms).

Measured on the host with the real InputManager driven by a fake ADC trace
(UP down at t=50, BACK pressed at t=1550, both ~X4 ADC averages):

chord = getHeldTime(), back/up = getHeldTime(BTN_BACK) / getHeldTime(BTN_UP):

    UP held 1500ms                      chord=1493   back=0      up=1493
    BACK pressed 100ms into UP hold     chord=1593   back=93     up=1593
    BACK released (UP still down)       chord=1643   back=100    up=1643
    UP released (all up)                chord=1650   back=100    up=1650

`chord=1593` is what a caller asking "how long has Back been held?" gets today;
93 ms is the truth. The chord column is identical before and after this patch.

THE FIX IN THIS PATCH
---------------------
Purely additive, so it can land without touching any consumer:

  * per-button buttonPressStartAt[]/buttonPressFinishAt[], stamped from
    pressedEvents/releasedEvents inside applyStateChange() — the single funnel
    every input style goes through — plus the two hold-emulating styles
    (updateConfirmBackHold / updateConfirmPowerHold) which synthesize their
    button bits after applyStateChange() and therefore stamp explicitly;
  * a new overload `getHeldTime(uint8_t buttonIndex)` with the same two-case
    shape as the existing accessor, applied to one button;
  * `BTN_COUNT` for the array bound;
  * the existing getHeldTime() is UNCHANGED, so no current caller moves.

Verified: identical `chord=` column for the pristine and patched SDK on the trace
above (host build, real InputManager.cpp, fake Arduino layer), and no new
compiler diagnostics (clang++ -std=gnu++2a -Wall -Wextra: the only warning,
`absInt` unused at FREEINK_CAP_TOUCH=0, is present before the patch too).

WHY THE NO-ARG SEMANTICS WERE NOT CHANGED INSTEAD
-------------------------------------------------
Redefining getHeldTime() to mean "the newest press" would silently retarget every
existing call in every SDK consumer, including continuous list-scroll repeat and
multi-stage keyboard holds, on hardware that cannot be regression-tested from a
patch. The chord timer is also legitimately what a few callers want (a two-button
chord's total duration). Adding the precise accessor and migrating callers one at
a time is reviewable; flipping the shared meaning is not.

CONSUMER MIGRATION (crosspoint-reader), for reference — not part of this patch
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Every call that asks about one specific button should move to the overload:

  src/activities/reader/EpubReaderActivity.cpp:451,462  Confirm  (bookmark / KOsync)
  src/activities/reader/EpubReaderActivity.cpp:476,483  Back     (file browser / home)
  src/activities/reader/XtcReaderActivity.cpp:105,112   Back
  src/activities/reader/TxtReaderActivity.cpp:64,71     Back
  src/activities/reader/EndOfBookOptions.cpp:56         Back
  src/activities/reader/EpubReaderBookmarksActivity.cpp:131  Confirm
  src/activities/home/FileBrowserActivity.cpp:193,230,280    Back / Confirm
  src/activities/home/RecentBooksActivity.cpp:60        Confirm
  src/activities/util/KeyboardEntryActivity.cpp:200,294,323,332  Up / Right / Confirm

Three call sites cannot migrate as-is; they need the button plumbed to them
first, or must stay on the chord timer:

  src/activities/reader/EpubReaderActivity.cpp:542 and
  src/activities/reader/XtcReaderActivity.cpp:140 ask "was the page-turn press a
  long one?" without knowing which of the four possible page-turn buttons fired
  (ReaderUtils::detectPageTurn collapses them), so they need the button plumbed
  out of detectPageTurn first.

  src/util/ButtonNavigator.cpp:70 (shouldNavigateContinuously) has no button in
  scope; onContinuous() knows the candidate set and would have to pass the
  matching one down.

diff --git a/libs/hardware/InputManager/include/InputManager.h b/libs/hardware/InputManager/include/InputManager.h
index be0d113..d012bf6 100644
--- a/libs/hardware/InputManager/include/InputManager.h
+++ b/libs/hardware/InputManager/include/InputManager.h
@@ -46,8 +46,24 @@ class InputManager {
   bool isDebouncePending() const { return lastState != currentState; }
 
   // Duration between the first button press and final release.
+  //
+  // NOTE: this is a single timer for the whole chord. It starts when a button
+  // goes down while nothing was down, and is NOT restarted by a button that
+  // joins an already-pressed chord. On boards whose buttons sit on independent
+  // pins (the X3/X4 ADC ladder puts UP/DOWN on BUTTON_ADC_PIN_2 and the four
+  // front buttons on BUTTON_ADC_PIN_1, so cross-group combinations are
+  // detectable), holding one button and then pressing a second makes the second
+  // button report the first one's elapsed hold. Use getHeldTime(buttonIndex)
+  // when the caller means "how long has THIS button been held".
   unsigned long getHeldTime() const;
 
+  // Duration of the current or most recent hold of one specific button:
+  // millis() - its press while it is down, else its own press-to-release span.
+  // Unlike getHeldTime(), an unrelated button already being held does not
+  // inflate the result. Returns 0 for an out-of-range index or a button that
+  // has not been pressed since begin().
+  unsigned long getHeldTime(uint8_t buttonIndex) const;
+
   // Duration of the current or most recent power-button hold.
   unsigned long getPowerButtonHeldTime() const;
 
@@ -58,6 +74,7 @@ class InputManager {
   static constexpr uint8_t BTN_UP = 4;
   static constexpr uint8_t BTN_DOWN = 5;
   static constexpr uint8_t BTN_POWER = 6;
+  static constexpr uint8_t BTN_COUNT = BTN_POWER + 1;
 
   // Pins. POWER_BUTTON_PIN stays constexpr (consumers reference it in pin-config
   // contexts) and is bound to the build's default device; the input code reads
@@ -212,6 +229,11 @@ class InputManager {
   unsigned long lastDebounceTime;
   unsigned long buttonPressStart;
   unsigned long buttonPressFinish;
+  // Per-button copies of the pair above, stamped from pressedEvents /
+  // releasedEvents in applyStateChange(). They back getHeldTime(buttonIndex);
+  // the chord-wide scalars keep getHeldTime() unchanged for existing callers.
+  unsigned long buttonPressStartAt[BTN_COUNT];
+  unsigned long buttonPressFinishAt[BTN_COUNT];
   unsigned long powerButtonPressStart;
   unsigned long powerButtonPressFinish;
   unsigned long confirmBackPressStart;
diff --git a/libs/hardware/InputManager/src/InputManager.cpp b/libs/hardware/InputManager/src/InputManager.cpp
index 482d263..802b406 100644
--- a/libs/hardware/InputManager/src/InputManager.cpp
+++ b/libs/hardware/InputManager/src/InputManager.cpp
@@ -63,6 +63,8 @@ InputManager::InputManager()
       lastDebounceTime(0),
       buttonPressStart(0),
       buttonPressFinish(0),
+      buttonPressStartAt{},
+      buttonPressFinishAt{},
       powerButtonPressStart(0),
       powerButtonPressFinish(0),
       confirmBackPressStart(0),
@@ -259,6 +261,15 @@ void InputManager::applyStateChange(const uint8_t state, const unsigned long cur
     powerButtonPressFinish = currentTime;
   }
 
+  // Per-button timers. Every input style funnels through applyStateChange(), so
+  // this is the one place that has to stamp them. Unlike the chord scalars above
+  // these are restamped for a button that joins a chord already in progress.
+  for (uint8_t i = 0; i < BTN_COUNT; i++) {
+    const uint8_t mask = static_cast<uint8_t>(1 << i);
+    if (pressedEvents & mask) buttonPressStartAt[i] = currentTime;
+    if (releasedEvents & mask) buttonPressFinishAt[i] = currentTime;
+  }
+
   currentState = state;
   // Keep lastState in sync with the committed state so isDebouncePending() is
   // meaningful on every input style. A no-op for the debounced ADC path (state
@@ -299,6 +310,10 @@ void InputManager::updateConfirmBackHold(const unsigned long currentTime) {
   if (emitConfirmClick) {
     pressedEvents |= (1 << BTN_CONFIRM);
     releasedEvents |= (1 << BTN_CONFIRM);
+    // These bits are synthesized after applyStateChange(), so its per-button
+    // loop never saw them. Mirror the chord scalars set above.
+    buttonPressStartAt[BTN_CONFIRM] = confirmBackPressStart;
+    buttonPressFinishAt[BTN_CONFIRM] = currentTime;
   }
 }
 
@@ -341,11 +356,17 @@ void InputManager::updateConfirmPowerHold(const unsigned long currentTime) {
 
   if (pressedEvents & (1 << BTN_POWER)) {
     powerButtonPressStart = confirmPowerPressStart;
+    // The shared pin went down before the BTN_POWER bit was synthesized; date
+    // the per-button timer from the physical press, as above.
+    buttonPressStartAt[BTN_POWER] = confirmPowerPressStart;
   }
 
   if (emitConfirmClick) {
     pressedEvents |= (1 << BTN_CONFIRM);
     releasedEvents |= (1 << BTN_CONFIRM);
+    // Synthesized after applyStateChange(); mirror the chord scalars set above.
+    buttonPressStartAt[BTN_CONFIRM] = confirmPowerPressStart;
+    buttonPressFinishAt[BTN_CONFIRM] = currentTime;
   }
 }
 
@@ -411,6 +432,18 @@ unsigned long InputManager::getHeldTime() const {
   return buttonPressFinish - buttonPressStart;
 }
 
+unsigned long InputManager::getHeldTime(const uint8_t buttonIndex) const {
+  if (buttonIndex >= BTN_COUNT) return 0;
+
+  // Still holding this specific button
+  if (isPressed(buttonIndex)) {
+    return millis() - buttonPressStartAt[buttonIndex];
+  }
+
+  // Never pressed since begin(): both stamps are 0, so the span is 0.
+  return buttonPressFinishAt[buttonIndex] - buttonPressStartAt[buttonIndex];
+}
+
